WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.517
What an LLM workload asks of a host.

2
00:00:02.797 --> 00:00:08.173
A typical web request spends a few milliseconds of CPU and returns in under a second.

3
00:00:08.453 --> 00:00:13.808
An LLM request is different in three ways, and they decide which hosting options fit.

4
00:00:14.088 --> 00:00:15.816
It is long and mostly waiting.

5
00:00:16.096 --> 00:00:22.496
A model call can take seconds to minutes, and almost all of that time your code is idle, waiting on the network.

6
00:00:22.776 --> 00:00:29.901
Platforms that bill or limit by CPU time treat that very differently from platforms that limit wall-clock time.

7
00:00:30.181 --> 00:00:31.163
It streams.

8
00:00:31.443 --> 00:00:35.859
A chat reply is a response body that keeps arriving for tens of seconds.

9
00:00:36.139 --> 00:00:42.688
The host has to support streamed responses, and its limits have to count streaming time in a way you can live with.

10
00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:44.781
It carries a valuable secret.

11
00:00:45.061 --> 00:00:51.760
The model API key can spend real money and, depending on its permissions, read your organization’s data.

12
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.544
It has to reach the server without ever touching the browser, the repository, or a log line.

13
00:00:57.824 --> 00:01:11.797
This lesson looks at three common hosting shapes through those three lenses, with one documented example each: serverless functions (Vercel Functions), edge workers (Cloudflare Workers), and containers (Google Cloud Run).

14
00:01:12.077 --> 00:01:19.523
These are examples, not endorsements; many other hosts follow the same shapes, and the questions you ask of them are the same.

15
00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:24.816
The numbers below were checked against each platform’s documentation on the review date.

16
00:01:25.096 --> 00:01:27.912
Limits change, so check them again when you deploy.
